# 8 The equations Basilisk solves {#ch:equations}

## One-fluid formulation

Water and air are each incompressible and Newtonian. Instead of solving two
sets of equations and matching them at the interface, Basilisk solves one set
over the whole domain, with density and viscosity that change across the
interface. Define the volume fraction (colour function)
$$
f(\mathbf{x},t) = \begin{cases} 1 & \text{in water (fluid 1)},\\ 0 & \text{in air (fluid 2)}.\end{cases}
$$
Each fluid particle keeps its identity, so $f$ is carried by the flow:
$$
\partial_t f + \mathbf{u}\cdot\nabla f = 0
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\partial_t f + \nabla\cdot(f\mathbf{u}) = 0 \ \text{ (because } \nabla\cdot\mathbf{u}= 0).
\qquad (8.2)
$$
The mixture properties are
$$
\rho(f) = f\rho_1 + (1 - f)\rho_2,\qquad \mu(f) = f\mu_1 + (1 - f)\mu_2.
\qquad (8.3)
$$
The momentum and continuity equations for the mixture are
$$
\begin{aligned}
\rho\left(\partial_t\mathbf{u}+ \mathbf{u}\cdot\nabla\mathbf{u}\right) &= -\nabla p
 + \nabla\cdot(2\mu\mathbf{D}) + \rho\mathbf{g}+ \sigma\kappa\,\delta_s\mathbf{n},
\\
\nabla\cdot\mathbf{u}&= 0,
\end{aligned}
\qquad (8.4, 8.5)
$$
with the rate-of-strain tensor $\mathbf{D}= \tfrac{1}{2}(\nabla\mathbf{u}+ \nabla\mathbf{u}^T)$, gravity
$\mathbf{g}$, surface tension coefficient $\sigma$, interface curvature $\kappa$,
interface normal $\mathbf{n}$ and $\delta_s$ a Dirac delta concentrated on the
interface.

These equations contain the jump conditions at the interface without
writing them separately: integrating (8.4) across a thin layer
around the interface gives the stress balance $[-p\mathbf{I} + 2\mu\mathbf{D}]\cdot\mathbf{n}
= \sigma\kappa\mathbf{n}$ (the Laplace pressure jump plus continuity of tangential
stress), and (8.5) gives continuity of normal velocity.

> **In the code: `two-phase-generic.h`**
