> #define rho(f) (clamp(f,0.,1.)*(rho1 - rho2) + rho2)
> #define mu(f)  (clamp(f,0.,1.)*(mu1 - mu2) + mu2)
> ~~~
>
> Equation (8.3), with $f$ clipped to $[0,1]$.

## The form used by the centered solver

`centered.h` writes the momentum equation per unit mass, with all body
and interfacial forces collected in one acceleration $\mathbf{a}$:
$$
\partial_t\mathbf{u}+ \nabla\cdot(\mathbf{u}\otimes\mathbf{u}) = \frac{1}{\rho}\left[-\nabla p
 + \nabla\cdot(2\mu\mathbf{D})\right] + \mathbf{a}.
\qquad (8.6)
$$
Because $\nabla\cdot\mathbf{u}= 0$, $\nabla\cdot(\mathbf{u}\otimes\mathbf{u}) = \mathbf{u}\cdot\nabla\mathbf{u}$, so
(8.6) is (8.4) divided by $\rho$. The solver stores
$1/\rho$ on faces as the *specific volume*
$\alpha = 1/\rho$ (the face field `alpha`) and $\rho$ at cell centres
(`rho`). The acceleration $\mathbf{a}$ is a face field too, so that it sits
where the pressure gradient sits ([chapter 14](14_acceleration) explains why that
matters).

## Conservative form and why it matters for two fluids {#sec:cons-form}

Using (8.2) and (8.3), the density obeys
$\partial_t\rho + \nabla\cdot(\rho\mathbf{u}) = 0$. Multiply (8.4) out and add
$\mathbf{u}$ times this mass equation to get the *conservative* momentum
equation
$$
\partial_t(\rho\mathbf{u}) + \nabla\cdot(\rho\mathbf{u}\otimes\mathbf{u}) = -\nabla p + \nabla\cdot(2\mu\mathbf{D})
 + \rho\mathbf{g}+ \sigma\kappa\delta_s\mathbf{n}.
\qquad (8.7)
$$
In exact arithmetic (8.4) and (8.7) are the same. In a
discrete scheme they are not. If mass ($f$, hence $\rho$) is moved with one
discrete flux and velocity with another, then at the interface the momentum
that leaves a water cell is not the momentum that arrives in the neighbouring
air cell. With a density ratio of 816, a small mismatch in the water
momentum flux becomes a large velocity in the air. [Chapter 12](12_momentum) shows
how `conserving.h` moves $\rho\mathbf{u}$ with the same fluxes as $f$ to avoid
this.

## Gravity as an interfacial force {#sec:reduced-derivation}

For a two-phase flow with constant densities in each phase, the gravity term
can be moved to the interface. Write $\mathbf{g}= \nabla(\mathbf{g}\cdot\mathbf{x})$ and use
$\rho = \rho_2 + (\rho_1 - \rho_2)f$:
$$
\begin{aligned}
-\nabla p + \rho\mathbf{g}&= -\nabla p + \nabla(\rho\,\mathbf{g}\cdot\mathbf{x}) - (\mathbf{g}\cdot\mathbf{x})\nabla\rho\\
 &= -\nabla p' - (\rho_1 - \rho_2)(\mathbf{g}\cdot\mathbf{x})\nabla f,
\end{aligned}
$$
where $p' = p - \rho\,\mathbf{g}\cdot\mathbf{x}$ is the *dynamic* pressure (total
pressure minus the hydrostatic pressure of each fluid). $\nabla f$ is zero
away from the interface, so gravity now acts only at the interface, as a
force of the form $\phi\nabla f$ with
$$
\phi = (\rho_2 - \rho_1)\,\mathbf{g}\cdot(\mathbf{x}- \mathbf{Z}),
\qquad (8.8)
$$
where the reference level $\mathbf{Z}$ only shifts $p'$ by a constant inside
each phase. Surface tension has the same form, $\sigma\kappa\delta_s\mathbf{n}=
\sigma\kappa\nabla f$ in the continuum-surface-force sense
\[[2](references)\], with $\phi = \sigma\kappa$. `iforce.h` adds every
such term as $\alpha\,\phi\,\nabla f$ on faces.

## Boundary conditions in the continuous problem

The continuous problem needs, on every boundary, conditions that make the
pressure Poisson problem solvable and the velocity well defined:

- a wall: $\mathbf{u}= \mathbf{u}_{\text{wall}}$ (no slip) or $\mathbf{u}\cdot\mathbf{n}= 0$
  with zero tangential stress (free slip);

- an inflow: $\mathbf{u}$ given;

- an outflow: a condition on pressure (for example $p = 0$) and a zero
  normal derivative of $\mathbf{u}$;

- a periodic pair: all fields equal on the two sides.

If all boundaries prescribe the normal velocity, integrating
$\nabla\cdot\mathbf{u}= 0$ over the domain shows that the prescribed fluxes must sum to
zero ($\oint\mathbf{u}\cdot\mathbf{n}\,dS = 0$). The pressure is then defined only up to a
constant. [Chapter 18](18_boundary) shows how each of these becomes a ghost-cell value.

